<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vakhtang VI of Kartli</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1675–1737</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vakhtang VI was king of Kartli in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, ruling as regent from 1703 and as king in 1716 and from 1719 to 1724 under Safavid overlordship. A scholar and lawgiver, he compiled a law code, organised the editing of the Georgian chronicles and set up the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> press in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a> in 1709. After allying with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a>, he lost his throne to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and in 1724 left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, where he died.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vakhtang-vi-of-kartli.jpg" alt="Vakhtang VI of Kartli" width="320" height="532" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: This work was first published in Georgia and is now in the public domain because its copyright protection has expired... · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vakhtang_VI_(miniature,_1700s).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
